---
title: "East Africa is preparing for El Niño. The test is what happens next."
summary: "A regional plan coordinates forecasts, health, transport and food security as East Africa braces for a very wet season."
category: Environment
date: 2026-11-13
author: "Fahamu News Desk"
---

East Africa's rainy-season warning is now a test of whether regional planning can reach people in time. Forecasts point to above-normal rain across much of the region, with eastern Kenya and central and southern Somalia facing a particularly strong signal.

On 6 November, the East African Community adopted a joint preparedness plan. Its five priorities are early warnings, health response, transport and infrastructure, food and livelihoods, and support for people displaced by flooding. The plan was announced on 9 November.

## Turning a forecast into action

The plan is built around a practical fact: floods and damaged roads do not stop at national borders. A washed-out crossing can interrupt trade and emergency access well beyond the community where it happens. The EAC says member states will share information and coordinate action around river basins, border areas and transport corridors.

Kenya told the regional meeting that risks had been mapped in 18 priority counties and that relief supplies had been positioned in advance. Separately, the Associated Press reported that Kenya's disaster operations centre had warned that as many as 1.5 million people could be affected, and that Nairobi had identified 172 possible evacuation shelters.

## Preparation has to include safe places to go

Early warnings matter most when people can act on them. AP's reporting from Nairobi described families who had already lost homes and belongings to floods, and residents facing the cost of finding safer housing. Clearing drains and keeping river buffers open can reduce exposure, but those steps need to account for the people living in flood-prone areas.

The EAC has set 25 November as the target for a regional disaster-information dashboard and 30 November for an implementation plan. The next measure of readiness is whether warnings, shelters, supplies and passable routes are available where the rain hits.

**Sources:** [East African Community preparedness plan](https://www.eac.int/press-releases/144-environment-natural-resources/3617-eac-ministers-adopt-regional-el-ni%C3%B1o-emergency-preparedness-and-response-plan) · [Associated Press reporting from Kenya](https://apnews.com/article/kenya-el-nino-nairobi-floods-ca89cbcfc9d9e88f59cbefe076559195)
